{
  "dom_id": "step-q120148",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14093672/assets/f43ffff0a3266e9d25073aa6b184da780b7fbfd74c4dbd1b55907dd3606e71f0.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14093672/assets/071b9aa71508200a513fad3b6d03a3b5ef8e18e74d5e1115ca130625420d01eb.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=1$ and $y=3.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =(y-1)(y-3) \\\\ {y}^{\u2032} & =(y-1)^{2}(y-3) \\\\ {y}^{\u2032} & =(y-1)(3-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=1$ and $y=3$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<1$ | $1<y<3$ | $y>3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\u2197$ | $\u2198$ | $\u2197$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=(y-1)(y-3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{\u2032}=(y-1)(y-3).$",
